"Internal Error ()\nciteturn1view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.rs/crate/sp1-gpu-logup-gkr/6.10.0\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: URL https://docs.rs/crate/sp1-gpu-logup-gkr/6.10.0 is not accessible via this tool.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://levs57.github.io/logup_.pdf)\nciteturn1view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 7; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://levs57.github.io/logup_.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 287\nL0@P0: Logup*: faster, cheaper logup argument for\nL1@P0: small-table indexed lookups\nL2@P0: Lev Soukhanov ∗\nL3@P0: May 2025\nL4@P0: Abstract\nL5@P0: Logup argument (in it’s modern GKR version, as described in\nL6@P0: eprint:2023/1284 paper) is a logarithmic derivative-based unindexed\nL7@P0: lookup argument. An indexed lookup argument can be constructed\nL8@P0: from unindexed one using standard trick.\nL9@P0: In this short informal note, we explain a different way of obtaining\nL10@P0: indexed lookup from logup, which does not commit any additional\nL11@P0: arrays of the size of the indexing array. That makes it particularly\nL12@P0: amenable for lookups in small tables (giving, to our knowledge, a first\nL13@P0: argument with this property).\nL14@P0: Additionally, this argument is not subject to numerator overflow\nL15@P0: issue that requires additional mitigation described in eprint:2024/2067.\nL16@P0: Improvements to SPARK / Lasso protocols are also discussed.\nL17@P0: 1 Introduction\nL18@P0: Logup argument, as described in [4] [5], is a following unindexed lookup\nL19@P0: argument: for two arrays X, T with values in some cryptographically large\nL20@P0: field F of characteristic p we wish to prove that X ⊂ T (as sets). For\nL21@P0: simplicity of exposition, also assume that Y ’s elements do not repeat.\nL22@P0: Denote |X| = n, |T| = m.\nL23@P0: Prover commits these arrays and additionally commits an array acc of\nL24@P0: size m which represents access counts - acc[i] is the number of times that\nL25@P0: the value T[i] is visited by X. Assume, additionally, that |X| < p, so these\nL26@P0: access counts never overflow.\nL27@P0: ∗\nL28@P0: [[alloc]init]. email:0xdeadfae@gmail.com\nL29@P0-1: 1Then, it is enough to check, for a random challenge c ∈ F that\nL30@P1: X\nL31@P1: 0≤i<n\nL32@P1: 1\nL33@P1: c − X[i]\nL34@P1: =\nL35@P1: X\nL36@P1: 0≤j<m\nL37@P1: acc[j]\nL38@P1: c − T[j]\nL39@P1: Soundness error of this argument is n+m\nL40@P1: |F|\nL41@P1: , and the check itself is nowadays\nL42@P1: usually done using GKR protocol as described in [5].\nL43@P1: Logup is sometimes used in an indexed lookup form; say as a drop\u0002in replacement for permutation argument lookups in Lasso [8] and [6]. In\nL44@P1: these cases, a standard reduction of indexed lookup to non-indexed lookup\nL45@P1: is typically used.\nL46@P1: Let table T be as before and let I be a set of indices |I|. We denote (I\nL47@P1: ∗T)\nL48@P1: the indexed lookup of table T with indices I (also called pullback further),\nL49@P1: defined as\nL50@P1: (I\nL51@P1: ∗T)[i] = T[I[i]]\nL52@P1: Standard reduction does the following:\nL53@P1: 1. (Assuming I, T already committed) commit to I\nL54@P1: ∗T, acc.\nL55@P1: 2. Get challenges c, γ.\nL56@P1: 3. Validate\nL57@P1: X\nL58@P1: 0≤i<n\nL59@P1: 1\nL60@P1: c − (I\nL61@P1: ∗T[i] + γI)\nL62@P1: =\nL63@P1: X\nL64@P1: 0≤jm\nL65@P1: acc[j]\nL66@P1: c − (T[j] + γj)\nL67@P1: Interpretation here is that we are actually doing an unindexed lookup\nL68@P1: argument of the set of pairs (I[i], I∗T[i]) in a table (j, T[j]), with random\nL69@P1: challenge γ used to fold a tuple into a single representative value.\nL70@P1: We are mostly interested in the following setting:\nL71@P1: 1. m << n.\nL72@P1: 2. Indices live in some ”small” subset of a field that can be efficiently\nL73@P1: committed to (maybe F is an extension field of some smaller base\nL74@P1: field, or maybe our commitment scheme is just more efficient for small\nL75@P1: elements).\nL76@P1: 3. Table T consists of large, essentially random elements of F (this fre\u0002quently happens, for example, in SPARK the table represents the eval\u0002uations of polynomial eqr\nL77@P1: ).\nL78@P1-2: 2In this regime, the logup argument has a significant overhead, commitment\u0002wise. In addition to the indexing array I, which is n small elements, the\nL79@P2: prover needs to commit the array of looked up values I\nL80@P2: ∗T, which is n large\nL81@P2: elements.\nL82@P2: Certain ad-hoc mitigations are available for elliptic curve commitments;\nL83@P2: but for hash-based schemes, this is a significant performance killer.\nL84@P2: We suggest an alternative protocol that doesn’t have these issues.\nL85@P2: Acknowledgement. Author thanks Yar Rebenko, Misha Komarov,\nL86@P2: Ron Rothblum, Srinath Setty and Liam Eagen for useful discussions that\nL87@P2: led to this work.\nL88@P2: 2 Notation\nL89@P2: 2.1 Multilinear polynomials\nL90@P2: We assume some familiarity of the reader with the multilinear setting. We\nL91@P2: refer to excellent book by Justin Thaler [10], and to foundational papers on\nL92@P2: sumcheck [3] and gkr protocol [2][9].\nL93@P2: We will use the following notation. For an array P of size 2k we will\nL94@P2: denote with the same letter the unique multilinear polynomial with val\u0002ues P(i0, ..., ik−1) = P[i0 + 2i1 + ... + 2k−1\nL95@P2: ik−1] on a boolean hypercube\nL96@P2: (i0, ..., ik−1) ∈ B\nL97@P2: k\nL98@P2: .\nL99@P2: We will frequently use the inner product. For two arrays P, Q of the\nL100@P2: same size 2kthe value\nL101@P2: ⟨P, Q⟩ =\nL102@P2: X\nL103@P2: 0≤i<2\nL104@P2: k\nL105@P2: P[i]Q[i] = X\nL106@P2: x∈Bk\nL107@P2: P(x)Q(x)\nL108@P2: Evaluation of a polynomial P in a point r = (r0, ..., rk−1) can be com\u0002puted as an inner product with the multilinear Lagrange kernel:\nL109@P2: P(r) = ⟨P, eqr⟩\nL110@P2: where\nL111@P2: eqr(x) = Y\nL112@P2: 0≤i<k\nL113@P2: (rixi + (1 − ri)(1 − xi))\nL114@P2: We will use the sumcheck protocol. Its internal workings are not very\nL115@P2: important here, what is important is that it is a claim reduction protocol\nL116@P2: that, for a multivariate polynomial U reduces the claim about it’s sum\nL117@P2: X\nL118@P2: x∈Bk\nL119@P2: U(x) = s\nL120@P2-3: 3to a claim about its value in a challenge point\nL121@P3: U(r) = s\nL122@P3: ′\nL123@P3: We will use the sumcheck to reduce the claim of the form ⟨P, Q⟩ to\nL124@P3: evaluation claims of individual multilinear polynomials P and Q.\nL125@P3: Our protocol will also have the form of claim reduction: we will avoid\nL126@P3: committing to I\nL127@P3: ∗T directly and instead we will commit to data that allows\nL128@P3: us to evaluate it in a point r.\nL129@P3: 2.2 Pullback and pushforward\nL130@P3: We will use the language of pullbacks and pushforwards. Let A, B be two\nL131@P3: field-valued functions on the sets n = {0...n − 1} and m = {0...m − 1},\nL132@P3: respectively, and let I be a mapping I : n −→ m.\nL133@P3: Then, as already said before, a pullback of B along I is defined as:\nL134@P3: I\nL135@P3: ∗B[i] = B[I[i]]\nL136@P3: We define a dual notion, called pushforward:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVec in std::vec - Rust (https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/vec/struct.Vec.html)\nciteturn1view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/vec/struct.Vec.html#method.extend_from_within\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 6780\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: ## 【1†Vec】\nL3: \nL4: ## 【2†std】1.99.1\nL5: \nL6: (58cf8f9c3 2026-10-13)\nL7: \nL8: ## 【1†Vec】\nL9: \nL10: ### 【1†Sections】\nL11: \nL12:   * 【3†Examples】\nL13:   * 【4†Indexing】\nL14:   * 【5†Slicing】\nL15:   * 【6†Capacity and reallocation】\nL16:   * 【7†Guarantees】\nL17: \nL18: ### 【8†Associated Functions】\nL19: \nL20:   * 【9†new】\nL21:   * 【10†with_capacity】\nL22:   * 【1